1
00:00:00,640 --> 00:00:05,064
Meet BlindPass: pay privately, use privately. It turns a payment into

2
00:00:05,074 --> 00:00:08,763
single use access credits, so using a digital service does not

3
00:00:08,773 --> 00:00:12,943
require a customer account. The first application is private technical search.

4
00:00:13,196 --> 00:00:16,704
This demo shows the working product, the cryptography behind it, and

5
00:00:16,714 --> 00:00:19,040
exactly where its privacy guarantees stop.

6
00:00:20,635 --> 00:00:23,816
A private payment is only one part of a private experience.

7
00:00:24,048 --> 00:00:27,049
A service can still attach your purchase to an account, session,

8
00:00:27,121 --> 00:00:31,207
or API key, and connect every request afterward. For people researching

9
00:00:31,217 --> 00:00:35,933
sensitive topics, that creates a lasting activity trail. BlindPass asks: can

10
00:00:35,943 --> 00:00:39,050
someone prove they paid without identifying their purchase every time?

11
00:00:40,646 --> 00:00:44,211
BlindPass separates buying access from using it. A payment authorizes a

12
00:00:44,221 --> 00:00:47,924
fixed batch of credits. Blind issuance lets the device receive valid

13
00:00:47,934 --> 00:00:52,056
credentials without revealing the final credentials to the issuer. Later, a

14
00:00:52,066 --> 00:00:55,320
search presents one credential. The service checks that it is valid

15
00:00:55,330 --> 00:00:59,046
and unspent, without requiring an invoice identifier at redemption.

16
00:01:00,645 --> 00:01:03,970
Let's walk through the actual application. On the buy screen, the

17
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:07,412
current offer is ten private searches for zero point zero one

18
00:01:07,422 --> 00:01:10,951
ZEC. There is no signup form and no customer profile to

19
00:01:10,961 --> 00:01:15,749
create. This recording uses the local simulation environment. The payment events

20
00:01:15,759 --> 00:01:19,009
are simulated; the credential cryptography is real.

21
00:01:20,641 --> 00:01:23,840
Creating the purchase produces a signed invoice and a visible payment

22
00:01:23,850 --> 00:01:28,117
state. For this demo, we use simulate payment confirmation. No funds

23
00:01:28,127 --> 00:01:31,122
are sent, and this is not an on chain payment demonstration.

24
00:01:31,381 --> 00:01:34,971
Real Zcash payments require the external scanner bridge described in the

25
00:01:34,981 --> 00:01:39,022
project, including address derivation and incoming payment detection.

26
00:01:40,627 --> 00:01:43,802
Once the purchase is confirmed, receive private credits runs the issuance

27
00:01:43,812 --> 00:01:47,873
protocol. The browser blinds its requests, verifies the issuer's proof, and

28
00:01:47,883 --> 00:01:52,003
unblinds locally. The wallet now holds ten usable credits. BlindPass uses

29
00:01:52,013 --> 00:01:56,233
Cloudflare's Privacy Pass implementation rather than inventing a new cryptographic primitive.

30
00:01:56,435 --> 00:01:59,077
The issuer does not receive these final credits during issuance.

31
00:02:00,639 --> 00:02:04,091
Now we search for privacy. The request spends one credential against

32
00:02:04,101 --> 00:02:08,253
the protected search API, returns results from the bundled technical corpus,

33
00:02:08,357 --> 00:02:10,782
and updates the balance from ten to nine. This is a

34
00:02:10,792 --> 00:02:15,190
working local search service. The access proof establishes permission to search,

35
00:02:15,315 --> 00:02:19,032
without carrying the invoice identifier or requiring an account login.

36
00:02:20,632 --> 00:02:24,218
Credits stay in an encrypted local wallet. The product includes encrypted

37
00:02:24,228 --> 00:02:28,327
backup and restore, purchase history, and recovery of interrupted issuance using

38
00:02:28,337 --> 00:02:32,443
saved state. That makes accountless access more practical, but custody matters.

39
00:02:32,662 --> 00:02:35,909
Clearing local data without a current backup can lose access. There

40
00:02:35,919 --> 00:02:39,070
is no account based password reset to reconstruct missing credits.

41
00:02:40,646 --> 00:02:44,525
The protocol lab makes the privacy claim inspectable. Alice and Bob

42
00:02:44,535 --> 00:02:47,622
each purchase the same ten credit batch and receive twenty real

43
00:02:47,632 --> 00:02:51,608
credentials in total. Both use a shared key, class, epoch, and

44
00:02:51,618 --> 00:02:56,086
expiry. We then redeem credentials in shuffled order. Shared configuration helps

45
00:02:56,096 --> 00:02:59,056
avoid giving each buyer a distinctive cryptographic label.

46
00:03:00,632 --> 00:03:05,200
Compare the two transcripts. Issuance records authorized batches and blinded requests.

47
00:03:05,419 --> 00:03:09,064
Redemption records token hashes, without an invoice field. There is no

48
00:03:09,074 --> 00:03:13,563
direct cryptographic matching identifier between them. The fifty fifty matrix illustrates

49
00:03:13,573 --> 00:03:16,961
equal priors for two buyers in this transcript only example. It

50
00:03:16,971 --> 00:03:19,057
is not a measurement of real world anonymity.

51
00:03:20,638 --> 00:03:24,162
The running demo also tests abuse. Reusing a spent credential returns

52
00:03:24,172 --> 00:03:28,374
token already spent. A forged credential returns invalid token. Attempts to

53
00:03:28,384 --> 00:03:32,272
change the issuer key, expiry, class, or key identifier are rejected

54
00:03:32,282 --> 00:03:35,542
by client policy. These results were captured from the application. They

55
00:03:35,552 --> 00:03:39,078
demonstrate specific enforcement checks, not an independent security audit.

56
00:03:40,630 --> 00:03:43,912
The privacy story has three distinct layers. Shielded Zcash protects payment

57
00:03:43,922 --> 00:03:47,364
information on the public chain. Blind issuance removes the direct cryptographic

58
00:03:47,374 --> 00:03:50,772
payment to usage link. Optional verified Tor transport in the CLI

59
00:03:50,782 --> 00:03:55,454
reduces source network exposure. Timing, browser behavior, query contents, and compromised

60
00:03:55,464 --> 00:03:59,108
devices can still reveal information. BlindPass does not promise total anonymity.

61
00:04:00,644 --> 00:04:04,299
For developers, the reusable idea is simple: charge for access without

62
00:04:04,309 --> 00:04:07,880
building a customer identity into every request. The repository includes a

63
00:04:07,890 --> 00:04:11,545
client SDK and server redemption middleware. Private technical search is the

64
00:04:11,555 --> 00:04:15,002
working starting point. Paid data APIs and private downloads are possible

65
00:04:15,012 --> 00:04:18,488
future applications; they are not presented as shipped integrations or existing

66
00:04:18,498 --> 00:04:19,081
customers.

67
00:04:20,631 --> 00:04:23,750
The next milestones are concrete. Connect and validate a real Zcash

68
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:27,749
scanner on testnet, complete deployment operations, and obtain independent security review.

69
00:04:27,926 --> 00:04:31,740
Stronger configuration transparency is also future work, especially for first use

70
00:04:31,750 --> 00:04:35,513
trust. Today's deliverable is a working accountless search and credential system,

71
00:04:35,619 --> 00:04:39,094
with its external payment integration requirements and limitations documented openly.

72
00:04:40,658 --> 00:04:45,488
BlindPass demonstrates a different relationship between payment and access. A service

73
00:04:45,498 --> 00:04:48,449
can verify that a request is paid for without asking the

74
00:04:48,459 --> 00:04:52,687
credential to identify the purchase behind it. Explore the demo, inspect

75
00:04:52,697 --> 00:04:56,443
the protocol, and help test the next integration. One credit proves

76
00:04:56,453 --> 00:04:59,005
access. It never needs to say who you are.

